CS 21 26.1 Lab Guide 2   Refactoring Guide and Function Implementation in RISC-V  <p>When mechanically converting C code to RISC-V assembly, apply the following rules. These rules make it easier to match each C statement with its corresponding assembly instructions.</p> <p>Rule 1 — Use exactly one operation per line</p> <p>Each line of C code should contain only one arithmetic operation. This creates a clearer correspondence between the C statements and the assembly instructions.</p> <p>Original:</p> <pre><code>int x = a + b + c;
</code></pre> <p>Convert to:</p> <pre><code>int x = a + b;
x = x + c;
</code></pre> <p>Rule 2 — Associate each variable with a register</p> <p>Rename each variable to show the register that will hold its value.</p> <p>Original:</p> <pre><code>int n;
</code></pre> <p>Convert to:</p> <pre><code>int n_s0;  // Register s0 holds the value of n
</code></pre> <p>For example, a variable named <code>sum</code> stored in register <code>s1</code> may be renamed to <code>sum_s1</code>.</p> <p>Rule 3 — Rename function parameters according to argument registers</p> <p>Function parameters should be renamed according to the RISC-V argument registers assigned to them. The first parameter uses <code>a0</code>, the second uses <code>a1</code>, and so on.</p> <p>Original:</p> <pre><code>int add(int x, int y, int z)
</code></pre> <p>Convert to:</p> <pre><code>int add(int x_a0, int y_a1, int z_a2)
</code></pre> <p>This naming convention makes it clear which RISC-V register contains each parameter.</p> <p>Rule 4 — Pass arguments using argument registers</p> <p>Each function call should pass its arguments using <code>a0</code>, <code>a1</code>, and the other RISC-V argument registers.</p> <p>Original:</p> <pre><code>f(a, b);
</code></pre> <p>Convert to:</p> <pre><code>f(a0, a1);
</code></pre> <p>Rule 5 — Copy function arguments into local variables</p> <p>At the beginning of a function, copy each argument into the variable or saved register that will hold its value.</p> <p>Original:</p> <pre><code>int f(int a0, int a1) {
    // Function body
}
</code></pre> <p>Convert to:</p> <pre><code>int f(int a0, int a1) {
    int s0 = a0;
    int s1 = a1;

    // Function body
}
</code></pre> <p>Rule 6 — Return the value through <code>a0</code></p> <p>A function's return value must be placed in <code>a0</code>. Ideally, do this near the end of the function.</p> <p>Original:</p> <pre><code>return 123;
</code></pre> <p>Convert to:</p> <pre><code>int a0 = 123;
return a0;
</code></pre> <p>Rule 7 — Convert each branch into its own <code>if</code>–<code>else</code> statement</p> <p>Rewrite an <code>if</code>–<code>else if</code>–<code>else</code> chain as nested <code>if</code>–<code>else</code> statements. Compute complex conditions separately before evaluating them.</p> <p>Original:</p> <pre><code>void f() {
    if (x + y &gt;= 0) {
        // First case
    } else if (x + y + z &gt;= 0) {
        // Second case
    } else if (will_crash() == 0) {
        // Third case
    } else {
        // Final case
    }
}
</code></pre> <p>Convert to:</p> <pre><code>void f() {
    int temp1 = x + y;

    if (temp1 &gt;= 0) {
        // First case
    } else {
        int temp2 = x + y;
        temp2 = temp2 + z;

        if (temp2 &gt;= 0) {
            // Second case
        } else {
            int temp3 = will_crash();

            if (temp3 == 0) {
                // Third case
            } else {
                // Final case
            }
        }
    }
}
</code></pre>
